                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "The Shadow Is Measurable", body: "Jung described the Shadow in 1912. He couldn't prove it existed in the brain. Now we can.\n\nSHADOW = IMPLICIT MEMORY + AMYGDALA-PREFRONTAL MISMATCH\n\nWhen you suppress an emotion — anger, grief, fear, shame — it doesn't disappear. It gets stored in subcortical networks: the amygdala (threat detection), basal ganglia (habit loops), and insula (body sensation).\n\nStudy: Ochsner & Gross (2005) — Suppressed emotions INCREASE amygdala reactivity while REDUCING prefrontal cortex regulation. Translation: the more you suppress, the more reactive your threat detection becomes AND the less your rational brain can manage it.\n\nStudy: Etkin et al. (2015) — Chronic emotional suppression shows measurable amygdala hyperactivity on fMRI. The shadow isn't metaphorical. It's a pattern of neural firing that persists and strengthens over time.\n\nThis is why engineers who \"don't do emotions\" are often the most emotionally reactive when triggered. The suppression didn't eliminate the emotion — it amplified it while removing conscious access to it."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Projection = False Positive Threat Detection", body: "Jung said projection is seeing your own disowned traits in other people. Neuroscience says: your brain is running a faulty threat model.\n\nThe brain is a prediction machine (Friston's Predictive Coding, 2010). It constantly generates top-down predictions about what will happen next and compares them to incoming sensory data. When there's a mismatch — a prediction error — the brain flags it for attention.\n\nShadow material creates mismatched priors. If you suppressed anger at age 7, your brain's model says \"anger = danger.\" Now, at 35, anyone who shows even mild assertiveness triggers a prediction error → amygdala fires → you perceive THREAT where none exists.\n\nThat's projection. It's not a spiritual concept. It's a Bayesian inference error caused by outdated training data.\n\nStudy: Internal Family Systems (IFS) research (Schwartz & Sweezy, 2019) shows that \"exiled\" emotional parts have measurable autonomic signatures — changes in heart rate variability and skin conductance. Shadow parts are real subsystems with real physiological markers.\n\nPRACTICAL IMPLICATION\nThe 90-second exposure technique from Module 7? Here's why it works neurologically: when you feel the emotion without adding a narrative, you allow the amygdala response to complete its cycle (approximately 90 seconds for the neurochemical cascade). The prefrontal cortex re-engages. The prediction model updates. The false positive gets corrected.\n\nJung called it integration. Neuroscience calls it memory reconsolidation. Same process."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Archetypes = Evolved Neural Hardware", body: "Jung's archetypes — Hero, Shadow, Anima/Animus, Self, Trickster — were always his most controversial claim. Universal patterns in the \"collective unconscious\"? Sounds like mysticism.\n\nExcept evolutionary neuropsychology found them.\n\nStudy: Panksepp's Affective Neuroscience — Identified 7 primary emotional systems (SEEKING, RAGE, FEAR, LUST, CARE, PANIC/GRIEF, PLAY) conserved across ALL mammals. These aren't learned. They're hard-wired subcortical circuits. They operate below conscious awareness. They organize behavior across species.\n\nThese map directly to Jungian archetypes:\n• SEEKING system → Hero archetype (drive to explore, achieve)\n• RAGE system → Shadow (suppressed aggression)\n• CARE system → Great Mother archetype (nurturing, bonding)\n• FEAR/PANIC → Abandoned Child archetype\n\nStudy: Knox (2003) reframed archetypes as early attachment schemas wired into orbitofrontal and cingulate circuits. Your brain comes pre-loaded with templates for how to relate to the world — just like an operating system comes with default drivers.\n\nStudy: Cross-cultural fMRI (Whalen et al.) shows universal facial expression recognition activates conserved neural networks regardless of culture. The hardware is the same. The software (culture) varies.\n\nJung was describing evolutionary firmware. He just didn't have the vocabulary for it yet."),
                ],
