                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Jung's Most Controversial Idea — With Math", body: "Synchronicity — meaningful coincidence without causal connection — was Jung's wildest claim. He developed it with Nobel physicist Wolfgang Pauli. Most scientists dismissed it.\n\nThen predictive coding theory came along.\n\nFriston's Free Energy Principle (2010): Your brain is constantly running a predictive model. It generates expectations and compares them to reality. When something happens that has very low probability in your model but very high personal significance — that's a synchronicity.\n\nIn Bayesian terms: a synchronicity is a high-information, low-probability event that your brain's pattern detection system flags as meaningful.\n\nYour brain isn't making it up. It's doing exactly what it's designed to do — detect patterns and assign significance to improbable correlations. The question isn't whether synchronicities are \"real.\" The question is whether the patterns your brain detects are noise or signal.\n\nStudy: Anterior cingulate cortex and insula activation during \"meaningful coincidence detection\" mirrors the neural signature of insight moments (the \"aha!\" experience). Your brain processes synchronicities the same way it processes creative breakthroughs."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Why Synchronicities Increase During Awakening", body: "People in active awakening (Stages 3-5) consistently report increased synchronicities. Skeptics say it's confirmation bias. The brain science offers a more interesting explanation.\n\nDuring intensive inner work — meditation, shadow processing, dark night — your brain's prediction model is being rebuilt. Old priors are dissolving. The DMN is quieting. The SN (salience network) is recalibrating.\n\nWhen the old model loosens its grip, your pattern detection becomes more sensitive. You notice connections your rigid old model would have filtered out. Not because the coincidences weren't happening before — but because your attention was locked into a narrower model.\n\nIt's like removing a spam filter that was blocking real emails. The emails were always there. You just couldn't see them.\n\nStudy: Alpha-gamma coupling research shows that states of relaxed alertness (alpha waves) combined with focused binding (gamma waves) produce peak pattern detection. This is exactly the brain state that meditation cultivates.\n\nJung and Pauli hypothesized that mind and matter share a common ordering principle (unus mundus). Modern complexity science (Atmanspacher & Fuchs, 2014) models this as lawful correlations emerging from shared contextual variables — no quantum mysticism required, just the mathematics of complex systems.\n\nWhether synchronicities are \"just\" your brain being a really good pattern detector, or whether they reflect something deeper about the structure of reality — the practical advice is the same: pay attention to them. They're high-value data points. Log them. Look for patterns across patterns. Test the hypotheses they suggest.\n\nYour brain is trying to tell you something. The question is whether you're listening."),
