                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Protocol 1: Focused Attention (10 min/day)", body: "WHAT: Sit. Pick an anchor — breath sensation at nostrils, heartbeat, or a neutral sound. Hold attention there. When mind wanders, note \"wandering\" and return. That's it.\n\nWHY IT WORKS: Each \"return\" strengthens the prefrontal-parietal attention network. It's a bicep curl for your CEN. The wandering isn't failure — it's the rep.\n\nNEURAL TARGET: Increased frontal theta + gamma synchrony. Stronger top-down prediction control.\n\nMETRIC: Count \"returns\" per session. Track over weeks. Lower returns = more stable attentional model.\n\nSHADOW LINK: Trains non-reactivity. When a shadow trigger fires, this is the muscle that lets you PAUSE instead of react.\n\nSTUDY: Lutz et al. (2004) — Gamma oscillations in trained practitioners. Jha et al. (2007) — 8 weeks of FA training improves attentional performance on standardized tests."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Protocol 2: Open Monitoring (10 min/day)", body: "WHAT: Sit. Don't focus on anything specific. Notice whatever arises — thoughts, sensations, emotions, sounds — without labeling them good or bad. Just observe.\n\nThe advanced version: become aware of awareness itself. \"I notice I am noticing.\"\n\nWHY IT WORKS: Decouples the DMN from the SN. Your \"Me\" circuit stops hijacking your \"What Matters\" circuit. You start seeing reality more clearly because your self-narrative isn't coloring everything.\n\nNEURAL TARGET: Reduced DMN hyperactivity. Balanced DMN-SN-CEN coupling.\n\nMETRIC: Emotional volatility score. Rate your emotional reactivity each morning (1-10). Track trend.\n\nSHADOW LINK: Allows suppressed material to surface without triggering the old suppress-or-react cycle. You see the shadow arising and just... watch it. That's integration.\n\nSTUDY: Brewer et al. (2011) — Reduced posterior cingulate activity even at REST. Your baseline state becomes less ego-dominated."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Protocol 3: Loving-Kindness (10 min, 3x/week)", body: "WHAT: Silently repeat phrases while holding someone in mind:\n\"May I be safe. May I be happy. May I be healthy. May I live with ease.\"\nStart with yourself. Then someone you love. Then a neutral person. Then someone difficult.\n\nWHY IT WORKS: Directly strengthens the empathy circuits (insula, anterior cingulate) that projection degrades. Reduces the amygdala's false-positive rate.\n\nNEURAL TARGET: Increased insula + ACC activation. Reduced amygdala reactivity to social stimuli.\n\nMETRIC: Relationship friction incidents per week. Track honestly.\n\nSHADOW LINK: The \"difficult person\" step is where projection gets healed. By generating genuine good-will toward someone who triggers you, you're updating the faulty Bayesian prior that says \"they are a threat.\"\n\nSTUDY: Weng et al. (2013) — 2 weeks produced measurable behavioral AND neural changes. Klimecki et al. (2014) — Compassion training reverses empathic distress patterns."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Protocol 4: Body Scan (15 min, 2x/week)", body: "WHAT: Lie down or sit. Slowly move attention from toes to head, noticing any sensation — warmth, tingling, tightness, numbness, nothing. Don't judge. Just note.\n\nWHY IT WORKS: Strengthens the insula — your brain's internal sensor. The insula is where \"gut feelings\" become conscious data. Shadow material often manifests as body sensation (tight jaw = suppressed anger, churning stomach = anxiety, heavy chest = grief) BEFORE it reaches conscious awareness.\n\nNEURAL TARGET: Right insula connectivity. Interoceptive accuracy.\n\nMETRIC: Interoceptive accuracy. Test: can you count your heartbeats for 30 seconds without touching your pulse? Accuracy improves with practice.\n\nSHADOW LINK: Your body is storing what your mind is suppressing. This practice reads the data your conscious mind has been ignoring.\n\nSTUDY: Farb et al. (2015) — Increased body-awareness accuracy and reduced alexithymia (inability to identify emotions). Hölzel et al. (2011) — Structural changes in interoceptive regions after 8 weeks MBSR.\n\nTHE 8-WEEK PLAN:\nWeeks 1-2: Protocol 1 (Focused Attention) daily, 10 min\nWeeks 3-4: Add Protocol 2 (Open Monitoring), alternate days\nWeeks 5-6: Add Protocol 3 (Loving-Kindness) 3x/week + Protocol 4 (Body Scan) 2x/week\nWeeks 7-8: Mix all four. Track all metrics. Compare to baseline.\n\nExpected outcomes (from meta-analyses):\n• 25-45% reduction in rumination\n• 15-30% faster emotional recovery from triggers\n• Measurable increase in gray matter density in key integration areas\n• Improved decision-making under uncertainty\n\nThese aren't promises. They're statistical averages from controlled trials with thousands of participants. Run the experiment on yourself and measure."),
